1. Aim
To understand Python functions and dictionaries and refactor previous programs into reusable tools.

2. Objectives
IT12: Create three functions: inspect(), tally() and report().
IT10: Refactor the Lab 04 program using functions.
IT8: Create a parts catalogue using dictionaries and sets.
IT6: Create a separate module named shoptools.py and import it into the main program.
3. Concepts Used
Functions and return statements
Parameters and arguments
Dictionaries and sets
Dictionary .get() method
Docstrings
Python modules and imports
Code refactoring
4. Functions Developed
inspect(dia) – Classifies a part as ACCEPT, REWORK or SCRAP.
tally(labels) – Counts the number of parts in each category.
report(shift, counts) – Generates a formatted shift report.
lookup(catalogue, code) – Finds a part diameter or returns a default value.
scrap_codes(catalogue) – Returns a set of rejected part codes.
5. Container Justification
List: Stores multiple inspection results.
Dictionary: Stores part codes and their diameters.
Set: Stores unique scrap part codes.
6. Inspection and Testing
Test functions with valid inputs.
Check empty input using tally([]).
Test missing dictionary keys using .get().
Check incorrect input types.
Verify that refactoring does not change program output.
7. Result
Successfully studied Python functions, dictionaries, sets and modules and converted repeated program operations into reusable tools.

8. Conclusion
Functions and modules improve code reusability, readability and maintenance. Dictionaries and sets make production data management more efficient.

